@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

A @emph{directed acyclic graph} (DAG) is a set of nodes connected by one-way
edges with no cycles: dependencies, prerequisites, build orders, and
workflow steps are all DAGs.  Drawing one by hand is tedious --- you move
boxes around, untangle arrows, and start over when you run out of room.

Dag Draw removes that work.  You describe the @emph{structure} (what connects
to what); it computes the @emph{layout} (where each node sits and how each
edge is routed) using the GKNV algorithm from the paper @emph{A Technique for
Drawing Directed Graphs} by Gansner, Koutsofios, North, and Vo --- the
algorithm behind Graphviz.  The result is a clean, top-to-bottom
hierarchical drawing.

Every drawing follows the same three steps:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Build} a graph, by mutating an empty one or from a declarative spec.
@item
@strong{Lay out} the graph, which assigns ranks, orders nodes to reduce edge
crossings, and computes coordinates and edge routes.
@item
@strong{Render} the graph to ASCII, SVG, or DOT@.
@end enumerate

Dag Draw works on acyclic graphs.  If you hand it a graph with cycles,
you can find the offending edges with @code{dag-draw-detect-cycles} (see
@ref{Detecting Cycles}).

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Dag Draw requires Emacs 26.1 or later and the @code{dash} and @code{ht} packages.

Clone the repository and put it on your @code{load-path}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/dag-draw.el")
(require 'dag-draw)
@end lisp

or, with @code{use-package}:

@lisp
(use-package dag-draw
  :load-path "/path/to/dag-draw.el")
@end lisp

