@chapter Tutorials

These walkthroughs are learning-oriented: follow them top to bottom and
you will end up with working drawings.  Once you are comfortable, the
@ref{How-To Guides} show how to accomplish specific tasks, and the
@ref{Reference} lists every function.

@menu
* Your First Graph::             Build, lay out, and render step by step.
* Building from a Spec::         Declare a whole graph at once.
@end menu

@node Your First Graph
@section Your First Graph

In this tutorial we will draw a small workflow with a branch and a merge,
and render it as ASCII art in the terminal.  By the end you will have a
finished drawing and will understand the build/lay-out/render rhythm that
every dag-draw program follows.

@strong{Prerequisites.} Dag Draw installed and loadable (see @ref{Installation}).

@strong{Step 1: Create an empty graph.}

@lisp
(require 'dag-draw)
(setq g (dag-draw-create-graph))
@end lisp

@code{g} now holds an empty graph that we will fill in.

@strong{Step 2: Add nodes.} Each node has an @emph{id} (a symbol you use to refer to
it) and a @emph{label} (the text shown in the box).

@lisp
(dag-draw-add-node g 'design "Design")
(dag-draw-add-node g 'build  "Build")
(dag-draw-add-node g 'test   "Test")
(dag-draw-add-node g 'ship   "Ship")
@end lisp

@strong{Step 3: Connect the nodes.} An edge points from one node id to another.
Here, @code{design} fans out to both @code{build} and @code{test}, which both feed into
@code{ship}.

@lisp
(dag-draw-add-edge g 'design 'build)
(dag-draw-add-edge g 'design 'test)
(dag-draw-add-edge g 'build  'ship)
(dag-draw-add-edge g 'test   'ship)
@end lisp

@strong{Step 4: Lay out the graph.}

@lisp
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
@end lisp

This is where the algorithm runs.  It places @code{design} at the top, @code{ship}
at the bottom, the two middle nodes side by side, and works out how to
route the edges.

@strong{Step 5: Render it.}

@lisp
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))
@end lisp

You should see exactly this:

@example
┌────────┐
│ Design │
└────┬───┘
     │
     ├─────────────┐
┌────▼──┐      ┌───▼───┐
│ Build │      │ Test  │
└───┬───┘      └───┬───┘
    │              │
    ├──────────────┘
┌───▼───┐
│ Ship  │
└───────┘
@end example

@strong{What you've built.} You described four nodes and four edges; dag-draw
chose the positions, split the edge out of @code{Design} with a @code{├} junction,
routed it around the boxes, drew @code{▼} arrowheads at the destinations, and
merged the two incoming edges of @code{Ship}.  You never specified a single
coordinate.

To declare a whole graph in one form instead of mutating it node by node,
continue to @ref{Building from a Spec}.  To understand what the lay-out
step actually does, see @ref{The GKNV Layout Passes}.

@node Building from a Spec
@section Building from a Spec

In this tutorial we will build a graph from a single declarative
specification with @code{dag-draw-create-from-spec}, and add a couple of
visual touches.  This is the style integrators prefer: the whole graph is
data.

@strong{Step 1: Write the spec.} A spec is a property list with @code{:nodes} and
@code{:edges}.  Each node is @code{(id :label "..." ...attrs)}; each edge is @code{(from
to ...attrs)}.

@lisp
(setq g
      (dag-draw-create-from-spec
       :nodes '((research :label "Research")
                (design   :label "Design" :ascii-marker "* ")
                (build    :label "Build")
                (review   :label "Review" :ascii-highlight t)
                (ship     :label "Ship"))
       :edges '((research design)
                (design build)
                (build review)
                (review ship))))
@end lisp

Two visual attributes appear here: @code{:ascii-marker} prepends a string to a
node's label, and @code{:ascii-highlight} draws the box with double lines.
(See @ref{Node Attributes} for the full list.)

@strong{Step 2: Lay out and render.}

@lisp
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))
@end lisp

The result:

@example
┌──────────┐
│ Research │
└─────┬────┘
      │
      │
┌─────▼──┐
│* Design│
└────┬───┘
     │
     │
┌────▼──┐
│ Build │
└───┬───┘
    │
    │
╔═══▼════╗
║ Review ║
╚════╦═══╝
     │
     │
┌────▼──┐
│ Ship  │
└───────┘
@end example

Notice the @code{* ~ marker on ~Design} and the double-line box around
@code{Review}.

@strong{What you've built.} You created a five-node pipeline from one data
structure --- no imperative @code{add-node~/~add-edge} calls.  Because the spec
is just a plist, it is easy to generate programmatically, which is how
packages such as org-gtd integrate dag-draw.

To highlight a node at render time instead of in the spec, see
@ref{Highlighting a Node}.  For the full spec grammar and validation
rules, see @ref{Constructing Graphs}.

