@node Querying a Graph
@section Querying a Graph

You can inspect a graph without laying it out or rendering it.  These
queries work as soon as the nodes and edges exist.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "A") (b :label "B") (c :label "C"))
         :edges '((a b) (a c) (b c))))

(dag-draw-node-count g)            ; => 3
(dag-draw-edge-count g)            ; => 3
(dag-draw-get-node-ids g)          ; => (c b a)
(dag-draw-get-successors g 'a)     ; => (c b)
(dag-draw-get-predecessors g 'c)   ; => (b a)
(dag-draw-get-source-nodes g)      ; => (a)
(dag-draw-node-label (dag-draw-get-node g 'a))  ; => "A"
@end lisp

@code{dag-draw-get-source-nodes} returns the nodes with no incoming edges ---
the roots of the DAG@.  For every edge in the graph, use
@code{dag-draw-graph-edges}; for the edges touching one node, use
@code{dag-draw-get-edges-from} or @code{dag-draw-get-edges-to}.

See @ref{Querying} for the complete list of query functions.

@node Detecting Cycles
@section Detecting Cycles

Dag Draw is meant for @emph{acyclic} graphs.  If a graph might contain a
cycle, @code{dag-draw-detect-cycles} tells you which edges close one: it
returns the back-edges (as edge structures), or @code{nil} if the graph is
acyclic.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "A") (b :label "B") (c :label "C"))
         :edges '((a b) (b c) (c a))))

(mapcar (lambda (e)
          (list (dag-draw-edge-from-node e)
                (dag-draw-edge-to-node e)))
        (dag-draw-detect-cycles g))
;; => ((c a))
@end lisp

On an acyclic graph the same call returns @code{nil}:

@lisp
(dag-draw-detect-cycles
 (dag-draw-create-from-spec
  :nodes '((a :label "A") (b :label "B"))
  :edges '((a b))))
;; => nil
@end lisp

Use the reported edges to decide which to remove or reverse before laying
the graph out.  For why dag-draw insists on acyclicity, see
@ref{Why DAG-only}.

@node Highlighting a Node
@section Highlighting a Node

To emphasize one node at render time --- for example, to show "you are
here" --- pass its id as the third argument to @code{dag-draw-render-graph}.
In ASCII output the selected node is drawn with a double-line box and its
bottom port becomes @code{╦}.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "Plan") (b :label "Code") (c :label "Test"))
         :edges '((a b) (b c))))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii 'b))
@end lisp

@example
┌───────┐
│ Plan  │
└───┬───┘
    │
    │
╔═══▼═══╗
║ Code  ║
╚═══╦═══╝
    │
    │
┌───▼───┐
│ Test  │
└───────┘
@end example

The same @emph{selected} argument also affects SVG (a glow effect) and DOT
(@code{style=bold}).  To bake the emphasis into the graph definition instead,
use the @code{:ascii-highlight} node attribute (see @ref{Node Attributes}).

@node Modeling Branches and Merges
@section Modeling Branches and Merges

Fan-out (one node leading to several) and fan-in (several leading to one)
need no special syntax --- just add the edges.  Dag Draw draws the split
and merge junctions for you.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "Collect Data")
                  (b :label "Clean")
                  (c :label "Analyze")
                  (d :label "Report"))
         :edges '((a b) (a c) (b d) (c d))))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))
@end lisp

@example
┌──────────────┐
│ Collect Data │
└───────┬──────┘
        │
    ┌───┴───────────┐
┌───▼───┐      ┌────▼────┐
│ Clean │      │ Analyze │
└───┬───┘      └────┬────┘
    │               │
    ├┬──────────────┘
┌───▼▼───┐
│ Report │
└────────┘
@end example

The @code{┴} marks where @code{Collect Data}'s edge splits toward the two middle
nodes, and the bottom rows show the two edges merging into @code{Report}.  For
how these junction characters are chosen, see
@ref{Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions}.

@node Producing SVG Output
@section Producing SVG Output

Pass @code{'svg} as the format.  The result is a complete SVG document as a
string, which you can write to a file or embed in a web page.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "Start") (b :label "End"))
         :edges '((a b))))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(with-temp-file "graph.svg"
  (insert (dag-draw-render-graph g 'svg)))
@end lisp

The document begins like this:

@example
<svg width="130.0" height="120.0" viewBox="-15.0 -35.0 130.0 120.0" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <defs>
    <marker id="arrowhead" ...>
@end example

Node appearance is controlled by the @code{:svg-*} node attributes and by the
SVG configuration variables (see @ref{Node Attributes} and
@ref{Configuration Variables}).

@node Producing DOT Output
@section Producing DOT Output

Pass @code{'dot} to emit Graphviz DOT source, which you can pipe to the @code{dot}
command or any DOT-aware tool.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "Start") (b :label "End"))
         :edges '((a b))))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'dot))
@end lisp

@example
digraph G @{
  node [shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];
  edge [color=black];

  a [label="Start"];
  b [label="End"];

  a -> b;
@}
@end example

@node Tuning Spacing
@section Tuning Spacing

Spacing is controlled by configuration variables.  For ASCII output the
relevant ones are @code{dag-draw-ascii-node-separation} (horizontal gap
between neighboring nodes, in characters) and
@code{dag-draw-ascii-rank-separation} (vertical gap between ranks, in rows).
Bind them around a render to change the density:

@lisp
(let ((dag-draw-ascii-node-separation 10)
      (dag-draw-ascii-rank-separation 3))
  (dag-draw-layout-graph g)
  (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))
@end lisp

For SVG, @code{dag-draw-svg-coordinate-scale} multiplies the coordinate
system.  See @ref{Configuration Variables} for every tunable and its
default.

@node Reading Laid-out Coordinates
@section Reading Laid-out Coordinates

After @code{dag-draw-layout-graph}, each node carries its computed position
and the graph has a bounding box.  This is useful when you embed a
drawing in a larger canvas.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "A") (b :label "B"))
         :edges '((a b))))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)

;; (min-x min-y max-x max-y)
(dag-draw-get-graph-bounds g)   ; => (0.5 -1.5 9.5 6.5)

(let ((n (dag-draw-get-node g 'a)))
  (list (dag-draw-node-rank n)      ; => 0
        (dag-draw-node-x-coord n)   ; => 5
        (dag-draw-node-y-coord n)   ; => 0
        (dag-draw-node-x-size n)    ; => 9.0
        (dag-draw-node-y-size n)))  ; => 3.0
@end lisp

See @ref{Struct Accessors} for every accessor.

@node Modifying an Existing Graph
@section Modifying an Existing Graph

Remove an edge with @code{dag-draw-remove-edge} and a node with
@code{dag-draw-remove-node}.  Re-run @code{dag-draw-layout-graph} afterward to
recompute positions.

@lisp
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec
         :nodes '((a :label "A") (b :label "B") (c :label "C"))
         :edges '((a b) (b c))))
(dag-draw-remove-edge g 'b 'c)
(dag-draw-remove-node g 'c)
(list (dag-draw-node-count g)    ; => 2
      (dag-draw-edge-count g))   ; => 1
@end lisp

